1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,554
Let's talk about this, the context object. There's almost

2
00:00:03,654 --> 00:00:06,631
always an output puzzle about it in problem one.

3
00:00:06,731 --> 00:00:09,452
The good news: three rules cover every case.

4
00:00:11,533 --> 00:00:15,099
Rule one. For a regular function, this isn't decided when you

5
00:00:15,199 --> 00:00:18,225
write the function. It's decided by how you call it.

6
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:21,597
Here f is called plainly, with nothing before a dot.

7
00:00:21,697 --> 00:00:25,322
In strict mode, that means this is undefined. Without use

8
00:00:25,422 --> 00:00:28,328
strict, it would be the window object instead.

9
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:30,472
So it prints undefined.

10
00:00:31,566 --> 00:00:35,870
Exam trap. The exam code often writes this question-mark-dot

11
00:00:35,970 --> 00:00:39,981
x. On undefined, that safely gives undefined. Plain this

12
00:00:40,081 --> 00:00:42,770
dot x would throw a TypeError instead.

13
00:00:45,866 --> 00:00:49,616
Rule two. When you call a function as a method, with an object

14
00:00:49,716 --> 00:00:53,590
before the dot, this is that object. So car dot show gives blue.

15
00:00:53,658 --> 00:00:56,861
But watch this. We copy the function into a variable

16
00:00:56,961 --> 00:00:59,846
s, and call s with nothing before the dot. It's

17
00:00:59,946 --> 00:01:03,086
the same function, but now it's a plain call again.

18
00:01:03,458 --> 00:01:06,581
So we get blue, and then undefined. The object that

19
00:01:06,681 --> 00:01:10,246
owns the function doesn't matter. Only the call site does.

20
00:01:13,325 --> 00:01:16,446
Rule three. Arrow functions don't have their own this. They

21
00:01:16,546 --> 00:01:19,177
use the this of the place where they were written.

22
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:22,810
This arrow was written at the top level of a classic script,

23
00:01:22,910 --> 00:01:26,470
where this is window. And we set window dot carColor to red.

24
00:01:26,550 --> 00:01:28,869
So calling it gives red. And even call,

25
00:01:28,969 --> 00:01:31,538
with a different object, doesn't change it.

26
00:01:31,916 --> 00:01:33,688
Red, and red again.

27
00:01:34,783 --> 00:01:37,959
Exam trap. Call, apply, and bind cannot change an

28
00:01:38,059 --> 00:01:42,171
arrow function's this. They're simply ignored for that purpose.

29
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:47,703
For regular functions, you can set this

30
00:01:47,803 --> 00:01:50,454
explicitly. Here intro uses this dot name.

31
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:53,655
call runs the function now. The first argument becomes

32
00:01:53,755 --> 00:01:56,090
this, and the rest are passed one by one.

33
00:01:56,183 --> 00:01:59,387
apply also runs it now, but takes the arguments

34
00:01:59,487 --> 00:02:02,059
as an array. A for apply, A for array.

35
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:05,326
bind doesn't run anything. It returns a new function with

36
00:02:05,426 --> 00:02:08,372
this locked in, and here the first argument locked in

37
00:02:08,472 --> 00:02:10,786
too. Then we call that new function later.

38
00:02:11,183 --> 00:02:12,955
Here's the output.

39
00:02:16,050 --> 00:02:20,300
Now the exam-style puzzle. We set window dot gameLocation to

40
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:22,838
U M D, and make an object with V A.

41
00:02:22,908 --> 00:02:25,283
f is a regular function, and g is

42
00:02:25,383 --> 00:02:28,808
an arrow, and both print this dot gameLocation.

43
00:02:28,908 --> 00:02:31,216
f is a plain call in strict mode, so

44
00:02:31,316 --> 00:02:35,096
this is undefined, and the optional chain gives undefined.

45
00:02:35,175 --> 00:02:37,745
g is an arrow written at the top level,

46
00:02:37,845 --> 00:02:40,211
so this is window, and that's U M D.

47
00:02:40,308 --> 00:02:44,456
And g dot call with loc? Arrows ignore call. Still U M D.

48
00:02:44,841 --> 00:02:47,597
undefined, U M D, U M D.

49
00:02:50,675 --> 00:02:55,855
For contrast, f is a regular function, so call and bind do work on it.

50
00:02:56,241 --> 00:02:58,013
Both print V A.

51
00:03:01,108 --> 00:03:04,875
Practice question 1. What does this print? Assume it's a classic

52
00:03:04,975 --> 00:03:07,776
script in a browser. Pause the video and try it.

53
00:03:16,075 --> 00:03:21,322
Blue, red, undefined, blue. car dot show is a method call, so this is car.

54
00:03:21,422 --> 00:03:26,525
car dot arrow is an arrow, and even inside an object literal it uses the

55
00:03:26,625 --> 00:03:31,583
outer this, which is window, so red. s is a plain call in strict mode,

56
00:03:31,683 --> 00:03:35,919
so undefined. And s dot call with car sets this back to car.

57
00:03:40,008 --> 00:03:44,228
Practice question 2. What does this print? Pause the video and try it.

58
00:03:52,508 --> 00:03:57,772
undefined, 10, 99, 10, 99. a is a plain strict call. b is an

59
00:03:57,872 --> 00:04:03,582
arrow, so it uses window, 10. c is bound to game, 99. apply can't

60
00:04:03,682 --> 00:04:09,304
change an arrow, so 10 again. And a dot call with game gives 99.

61
00:04:13,375 --> 00:04:17,487
Recap. For a regular function, this is the object before the dot, and

62
00:04:17,587 --> 00:04:21,699
a plain call in strict mode gives undefined. For an arrow, this comes

63
00:04:21,799 --> 00:04:25,666
from where it was written, which at the top level is window. Call

64
00:04:25,766 --> 00:04:29,634
and apply run the function now with a chosen this. Bind returns a

65
00:04:29,734 --> 00:04:32,931
new function. And none of them change an arrow's this.

